{"reference":"Nehemiah 4","book":"Nehemiah","chapter":4,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Reading the Conflict Carefully","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Sanballat, Tobiah, and the other opponents are presented as regional political and kinship interests affected by Jerusalem’s fortification. Their hostility belongs to this narrated conflict and should not be turned into a timeless ethnic feud or used to vilify modern neighboring peoples. Likewise, the builders’ weapons and defensive arrangements answer the threat described in this chapter; the passage does not independently authorize religious militias, preemptive violence, or treating present-day critics as enemies of God.","verse_refs":["Nehemiah 4:1-8","Nehemiah 4:7-23"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_nehemiah_004_2fec540f1373c8cb4fdafbd4"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The Wider Building Story","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The wider account portrays Jerusalem’s rebuilding as shared work by varied households, priests, craftspeople, officials, and regional workers, while opposition takes forms such as ridicule, threat, accusation, and intimidation. The first-person voice in this part of the book gives the account an immediate perspective on building, prayer, and conflict, though the book as a whole also includes third-person narrative, lists, material about Ezra, and editorial transitions.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_nehemiah_004_b2bee5d8a3c631553c38f2a9"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}